<description>
Project maroon markers into adjacent grey or pink 3x3 squares, erasing grey squares afterward.

Input structure
The grid has a dominant background, solid 3x3 grey and/or pink squares, and maroon marker pixels or segments directly touching one side of exactly one square.
Rule
Grey squares are erased; their adjacent maroon markers stay in place and stamp the nearest edge cell inside the grey square. Pink squares stay pink; their adjacent maroon markers disappear and stamp the corresponding cell on the square's middle row or middle column.
Key insight
The side touched by a marker determines the projection: grey uses the near edge, while pink uses the centerline, preserving the marker's coordinate along that side.
</description>
<annotation>
{
  "type": "transform_in_place",
  "src": "maroon marker pixels or segments adjacent to solid grey or pink 3x3 square objects",
  "transform": "erase grey squares after stamping maroon into each touched near-edge cell; keep grey-adjacent markers in place; keep pink squares, remove pink-adjacent markers, and stamp their maroon cells onto the matching middle row or column of the pink square"
}
</annotation>
<generation_spec>
```yaml
spec:
  grid: 9-15 rows x 9-15 cols, roughly square [observed]
  background: azure (8)
  colors_used: background + grey(5) + pink(6) + maroon(9); not all required (pink-only is valid)
  squares:
    count: 1-7 total; 0-3 grey + 0-4 pink, at least 1 of either
    shape: solid 3x3 of a single color (grey or pink)
    placement: non-overlapping; min gap >=1 cell between any two square bboxes
    may touch grid boundary (markers never on boundary-blocked sides)
  markers:
    color: maroon (9), single pixels in cells orthogonally adjacent to exactly one square
    per_square: 1-5 markers across 1-3 sides; each side has 1-3 of 3 possible slots filled
    each marker touches exactly one square (no ambiguity)
    pink_projection: each pink-adjacent marker is handled independently. Left/right-side markers stamp the square's middle column at the marker's row; above/below-side markers stamp the square's middle row at the marker's column. A marker centered on its side therefore stamps the square center. Multiple markers from any sides simply union their projected cells; there is no side priority.
  transform:
    grey_squares: erased to background; markers stay in place and stamp the nearest edge cell of the former square (same row for left/right, same col for above/below)
    pink_squares: preserved; adjacent markers are removed, then replaced by the union of independent centerline projections according to pink_projection

generation_strategy:
  approach: output-first
  steps:
    1. Choose grid size H x W in [9..15].
    2. Choose number of grey (0-3) and pink (0-4) squares, total >=1.
    3. Place 3x3 squares on the grid with >=1 cell gap between bboxes. For each square, leave >=1 cell margin on sides that will receive markers.
    4. For each square, choose 1-3 sides to have markers (avoid sides blocked by grid boundary). For each chosen side, fill 1-3 of the 3 adjacent slots with maroon. Verify each slot is not adjacent to any other square.
    5. Build the output grid (pink squares present, grey squares erased to bg, maroon stamped at projected target cells, grey-adjacent markers remain, pink-adjacent markers removed).
    6. Derive input from output. For pink squares, restore full 3x3 pink, remove interior maroon stamps, place maroon at the original marker positions. For grey squares, fill 3x3 with grey, remove interior maroon stamps (markers already present at source).
```
